\section{Introduction}
The global threat of chemical warfare remains a critical public health concern. Understanding the combustion properties of deactivated CWAs is essential for preparedness and hazardous material management.

\section{Chemical Properties}
Common CWAs include sarin, VX, and mustard gas. These agents possess varying volatility ratings and oxidation potentials. Their decomposition products (sarin, chloromethane, hydrogen chloride) exhibit distinct kinetics.

\section{Phase Transformations}
When heated, most CWAs transition from solid to gas phase. The critical factor determining stability lies in the concentration of the active species.

\section{Combustion Calorimetry}
Samples were subjected to controlled heating in a bomb calorimeter. Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) measured temperature profiles.
\end{section}

\section{Experimental Procedures}
Thermal decomposition was monitored using a calibrated differential scanning calorimeter.
\end{section}

\section{Results and Discussion}
Combustion products were analyzed for decomposition products using gas chromatography.
